If you modify Merit Order and let people use it over a network, section 13 of the license asks you to offer
those users the source code of your modified version. The console's "Source" link points at it: set
console.source_url to your own repository.

Third-party components keep their own licenses. Python packages installed in the container images carry their
license files in each package's .dist-info directory under /app/.venv (and /opt/skypilot in the -gpu image).

The console bundles React, Mantine and other packages, mostly MIT-licensed. The build writes their licenses and
notices to third-party-notices.txt next to the bundle, and the gateway serves it at /ui/third-party-notices.txt
(linked from the console's footer).

The -compressor image installs PyTorch from PyPI, which on Linux brings NVIDIA's CUDA libraries as Python wheels
(nvidia-cublas, nvidia-cudnn-cu13, nvidia-nccl-cu13 and others, under /app/.venv). Those are NVIDIA's software,
under NVIDIA's own license terms (the NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit End User License Agreement and the cuDNN Software
License Agreement, among others), not the AGPL; each wheel's license is in its .dist-info directory. The other
images don't include them.
